 "post": {
  "title": "5 Signs You're Studying the Way That Feels Good (Not the Way That Works) #shorts",
  "description": "How many did you check? Tell me in the comments. Send this to the friend with the yellow textbook. Rosa's exam is Friday and she has highlighted the whole book. In 'The Generation Effect: Delineation of a Phenomenon' (Journal of Experimental Psychology: Human Learning and Memory, 1978), Norman Slamecka and Peter Graf had people either read word pairs (rapid – fast) or generate the second word themselves from a rule and its first letter (rapid – f_). Across five experiments, words people generated were remembered better than words they only read. Psychologists call it the generation effect: making your brain fill in the blank is the point. Cover your notes and test yourself. #psychology #shorts #studytips #generationeffect #overthinking",
  "tags": [
   "generation effect",
   "study tips",
   "how to remember",
   "Slamecka Graf 1978",
   "psychology"
  ]
